SPH examples

cavity_flow/in.cavity_flow : shear-driven laminar flow in a 2d square cavity
heatconduction/in.sph_heat_conduction_2d : heat conduction in a 2d bar of stationary particles
heatconduction/in.sph_heat_conduction_3d : heat conduction in a 3d bar of stationary particles
shock_tube/in.shock2d : quasi-1d shock tube problem in 2d
shock_tube/in.shock3d : quasi-1d shock tube problem in 3d
water_collapse/in.water_collapse : collapse of a 2d water column in a container with an obstacle

Some of these inputs run only a small number of time steps by default,
so that they can also be used for regression testing.  For those, the
number of time steps is set with the index style variable "nsteps",
which can be changed from the command line with the -var flag:

shock_tube/in.shock3d : default 100, use "-var nsteps 400" to run until
    t = 20, which is needed to compare with the exact solution using the
    compare_exact.gpl gnuplot script
water_collapse/in.water_collapse : default 2000, use "-var nsteps 50000"
    to follow the collapse of the water column until t = 10

Example: lmp -in in.water_collapse -var nsteps 50000

See the SPH how to page in the LAMMPS documentation,
https://docs.lammps.org/Howto_sph.html
for a detailed explanation of these examples.
